{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-fable-5-1","id":"msg_011CfPsjTfpN2ngVzRxjRt5J","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Done. `/app/answers.json` is written and valid, using the full budget of 40 runs.\n\n**Key finding.** The notes' rule eta*(N) = 3.877·N^-0.381 is wrong because every notebook sweep sat on D = 20N, confounding model size with training length. Sweeps at fixed N and fixed D separate the two effects. The optimal peak learning rate follows lr* ∝ N^-0.15 · D^-0.20 (both exponents ±0.007). The claim that muP makes the optimum independent of training length is refuted: at 1000 tokens/param the optimum is about 2.2x lower than at 20 tokens/param for the same model. A joint fit of all 65 runs with a shared asymmetric loss-vs-lr penalty reproduces every sweep to seed-noise level (~0.009 nats).\n\n**Answers.**\n\n| Q | Answer | Basis |\n|---|---|---|\n| q1 | log10 lr* = -2.755 (lr ≈ 0.00176) | power law extrapolated to N=1e9, D=2e10 |\n| q2 | log10 lr* = -3.092 (lr ≈ 0.00081) | same law at D=1e12 |\n| q3 | 0.027 nats excess | lr_prod is 1.8x above the optimum; penalty from the fitted shape |\n| q4 | [-3.092, -2.640] | q2 shifted by g·log10(8) for the known-unknown g in [0, 0.5] |\n| q5 | refuted | 10x N lowers lr* by only 29-30%, not 35% |\n| q6 | undetermined | 0.00157 lies inside the q4 range, so it depends on g |\n| q7 | B (0.00108) | predicted optimum at D=2e11 is ≈0.0011 |\n| q8 | 2.406 nats | loss law L = 1.71 + 533·N^-0.33 + 3609·D^-0.37, residuals 0.003 nats |\n\n**Caveats.** q1 to q3 involve extrapolating 0.5 decades in N and 1.5 decades in D beyond the lab range, relying on the manual's scale-consistency guarantee. Different penalty-shape models moved the extrapolated optimum by about 0.02 in log10; the dense sweep at N=1.2e8 confirmed the smooth asymmetric shapes, which I averaged. Intervals are degenerate except q4, per the manual's grading rules."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":